  "answer": "I'll treat this as a bug fix and start by reading the playbook and the principles it depends on.The storefront price check compares the start date against itself when an end date is present, and product creation writes the end date into the start field. I'll lock that in with a failing test, then fix both.A dated special never reached the storefront because product creation stored the end date in the start-date field, and `ProductPriceUtils` then required that start date to be both before and after today. The end date is now stored on the end-date field, and the price check compares that end date with today. Specials with no dates are unchanged. Fix Root Causes kept the edit on those two assignments. Prove It Works and Test Behavior Not Implementation added tests for the discounted amount. Sequence Verifiable Units ran the start-and-end case red before the fix and green after. Laziness Protocol left the unused `hasDiscount` method alone.\n\nVERIFICATION: {\"ran\": [\"mvn -pl sm-core -am -Dtest=ProductPriceUtilsDiscountTest -DfailIfNoTests=false test\", \"mvn -pl sm-core,sm-shop -am -Dtest=ProductPriceUtilsDiscountTest,PersistableProductAvailabilityMapperTest -DfailIfNoTests=false test\"], \"result\": \"passed\", \"could_not_verify\": [\"live admin and storefront REST calls against a running shop\"], \"missing\": [\"a documented local shop and database startup step\"]}",
